# Ship workflow (musician-ai-studio)

**Production = `main` branch + nabadai.com + TestFlight.** Daily work goes on the **`staging` branch** and staging Vercel preview API (see `staging-vs-production.mdc`). Do NOT push to `main` / deploy production / install TestFlight builds until the user has previewed on the simulator and approved.

## Staging vs production

- **`staging` branch + `npm run sim`**: bundled frontend + **staging API** — safe for backend and frontend iteration.
- **`main` branch push**: production web + API — affects live users and TestFlight (same backend once they refresh web; TestFlight uses bundled frontend until a new build).
- **Supabase** is still shared across staging/production API until a staging Supabase project is configured in Vercel Preview env vars. SQL migrations on the production DB still affect live testers — call that out before running SQL.

The iOS simulator loads **bundled local `www/`** and, with `npm run sim`, talks to the **staging** API — not nabadai.com.

## Step 1 — Preview on the simulator (default after any app change; do NOT deploy yet)

When you finish a task that changes tracked project files (`www/`, `src/`, root `index.html` / `styles.css`, etc.), preview it on the simulator **before ending the turn**, without committing or pushing:

1. **`npm run sim`** — bakes **staging** API env, syncs `www` into the iOS shell, builds for the booted simulator (default `iPhone 17 Pro`), installs, and launches. (Equivalent: `npm run env:staging && sh ios/App/scripts/run-ios-sim.sh`.) The physical-iPhone `run:ios` does NOT update the simulator and vice-versa — each needs its own build.
2. Take a simulator screenshot (`xcrun simctl io booted screenshot /tmp/x.png`) and show the user. **Then stop and wait for approval.** Do not git-push to `main` or `run:ios` with production env in the same turn unless the user already said to ship.

## Step 2 — Ship to production (ONLY after the user approves)

Once the user approves the simulator preview (e.g. "approved", "push it", "ship it"):

1. **`git status`** — confirm what changed.
2. **`npm run verify:no-producer-on-main`** — must pass before any merge/push to `main` (blocks accidental Producer ship).
3. **`npm run stamp:build`** — stamps a fresh, unique build id into the cache-busting asset URLs (`styles.css?v=…`, `src/app.js?v=…`) and the `APP_BUILD` marker, **and** mirrors everything into `www/` (it runs `sync-www` internally). The static `?v=` is what busts the WKWebView (native) and browser/CDN caches; without a bump the device/web keep serving stale code (the recurring "I don't see my change" problem). The id also shows in the faint on-screen build marker, so you can confirm a device got the new build.
4. **`git add`** the relevant paths (including the stamped `index.html`, `src/app.js`, and their `www/` mirrors) and **`git commit -m "…"`** with a short, accurate message (full sentences, no fluff).
5. **`git push origin main`** — production deploy (Vercel + nabadai.com). Merge from `staging` first unless the user explicitly ships a hotfix on `main`.
6. **`npm run env:production`** then **`npm run run:ios`** — syncs `www` with **production** API baked in, builds for the connected iPhone, installs, and launches (no Xcode debugger). Do not stop at `npx cap sync ios` unless `run:ios` fails (no device, signing error, etc.); report the failure and what succeeded.
7. **When the user says TestFlight / ship iOS / push to main and TestFlight** — also archive + upload in the same pass (do NOT tell them to open Xcode manually):
   - `NABAD_API_ENV=production npm run sync:ios`
   - `cd ios/App && xcodebuild -scheme App -configuration Release -destination 'generic/platform=iOS' -archivePath build/App.xcarchive archive -allowProvisioningUpdates`
   - `xcodebuild -exportArchive -archivePath build/App.xcarchive -exportOptionsPlist ExportOptions.plist -exportPath build/export -allowProvisioningUpdates`
   - Report version/build from the archive (`CFBundleShortVersionString` / `CFBundleVersion`) and that upload succeeded.
8. If `run:ios` changes tracked files under `ios/`, commit and push those too in the same pass.

9. **After every ship to `main` / TestFlight / production** — merge **`main` → `staging`** in the same pass (no need to ask). Push `staging` so daily dev never falls behind what testers have. Resolve conflicts on `staging`; do not leave staging missing commits that only landed on `main`.

10. **After step 9** — run **`npm run sync:producer -- --push`** so `feature/nabad-producer` gets every fix that landed on `main`. Sami's Producer phone branch must never fall behind production code.

If push fails, fix or report and do not claim success. If build/launch fails after a successful push, say so clearly — web deploy may still be live.
